# Curated list of public DoH/DNS-over-HTTPS resolver addresses, one per
# line, consumed by block_doh_ips() in gargoyle_firewall_util.sh when
# firewall.@defaults[0].block_doh_ips=1.
#
# This is deliberately a small, conservative subset of the 40+ providers in
# plugin-gargoyle-doh's own provider list
# (package/plugin-gargoyle-doh/files/usr/lib/https-dns-proxy/*.json).
#
# That directory's "bootstrap_dns" field is NOT reliably the provider's own
# DoH service address -- it is the IP used to resolve the provider's *own*
# hostname (e.g. several smaller/regional providers list Cloudflare's
# 1.1.1.1 as their bootstrap purely to look up their own domain, not because
# they run on 1.1.1.1 themselves). Treating every bootstrap_dns value as a
# blockable resolver address would be wrong for those entries and risks
# blackholing traffic unrelated to DoH.
#
# Only operators below are ones whose bootstrap_dns is verifiably their own
# dedicated public-resolver anycast address (bootstrap == service, confirmed
# against each provider's own JSON template/help_link, and matching Gargoyle's
# own shipped https-dns-proxy default instances for Cloudflare/Google -- see
# package/https-dns-proxy/files/etc/config/https-dns-proxy). Adding another
# provider here requires the same verification, not just copying its
# bootstrap_dns value.

# Cloudflare (com.cloudflare-dns.json)
1.1.1.1
1.0.0.1
2606:4700:4700::1111
2606:4700:4700::1001

# Google (google.dns.json)
8.8.8.8
8.8.4.4
2001:4860:4860::8888
2001:4860:4860::8844

# Quad9 (net.quad9.json)
9.9.9.9
149.112.112.112
2620:fe::fe
2620:fe::9

# OpenDNS / Cisco (com.opendns.doh.json)
208.67.222.222
208.67.220.220

# AliDNS (com.alidns.dns.json)
223.5.5.5
223.6.6.6
2400:3200::1
2400:3200:baba::1

# AdGuard (com.adguard.dns.json)
176.103.130.130
176.103.130.131
